1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:02,394
An atom never has to guess.

2
00:00:02,844 --> 00:00:05,067
You do. All day.

3
00:00:05,917 --> 00:00:10,552
An atom is held together for free.
Nothing it could do would change its fate.

4
00:00:11,002 --> 00:00:14,946
It carries information, but a model would
cost it something and buy it nothing.

5
00:00:15,796 --> 00:00:19,755
A bacterium is different: the sugar
drifts, and it can swim or tumble.

6
00:00:20,205 --> 00:00:25,585
Our hypothesis: knowing starts to pay when a
changing world can ruin you, and you have a move.

7
00:00:26,435 --> 00:00:30,266
But knowing is never free.
The bill has three parts.

8
00:00:30,716 --> 00:00:32,688
Memory: you pay to keep what you know.

9
00:00:33,138 --> 00:00:37,843
Mistakes: when the world moves and
you don't. Late counts as wrong.

10
00:00:38,293 --> 00:00:40,281
Checking: every look costs something.

11
00:00:40,731 --> 00:00:45,214
Look too often, you go broke.
Too rarely, you're wrong.

12
00:00:45,664 --> 00:00:49,835
The faster the world changes, the more
often to look, and the sooner to forget.

13
00:00:50,685 --> 00:00:54,582
Same bill at every scale.
Only the currency changes.

14
00:00:55,032 --> 00:01:00,837
A cell pays in heat. You, in
attention. A company, in money.

15
00:01:01,687 --> 00:01:03,605
Go bigger, and light gets in the way.

16
00:01:04,055 --> 00:01:07,611
Light takes about a hundred
thousand years to cross a galaxy.

17
00:01:07,611 --> 00:01:11,691
From one center, you could only hold
what barely changes in that time.

18
00:01:12,141 --> 00:01:16,107
So big organizations may need
to split into local parts.

19
00:01:16,107 --> 00:01:19,435
Whether real ones do,
for this reason, is open.

20
00:01:19,885 --> 00:01:22,361
A star holds itself
together, with no model.

21
00:01:23,211 --> 00:01:25,241
And at the far end: the black hole.

22
00:01:25,691 --> 00:01:29,446
The most memory physics allows for its
size, and almost none of it readable.

23
00:01:29,896 --> 00:01:33,179
From outside, you read
mass, spin and charge.

24
00:01:33,629 --> 00:01:37,647
On current physics, the rest returns
scrambled, far too late to matter.

25
00:01:38,097 --> 00:01:41,346
In this theory, it records the
past, and predicts nothing.

26
00:01:42,196 --> 00:01:46,537
Two frozen ends. Everything that
has to keep up lives in between.

27
00:01:47,387 --> 00:01:53,433
Here's the bet: strongly selected systems, cells to
cities, sit near the balance point their world predicts.

28
00:01:53,883 --> 00:02:00,006
Nobody has measured all of it on one
system yet. Untested. Not refuted.

29
00:02:00,856 --> 00:02:03,125
We've written down
what a fair test needs.

30
00:02:03,575 --> 00:02:06,478
Test it. Break it, if you can.
